1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
The nerve center of America's fight against crime and terrorism.

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:10,000
Futuristic equipment. Cutting-edge technologies.

3
00:00:10,001 --> 00:00:15,000
Now, the FBI Crime Lab on Modern Marvels.

4
00:00:15,001 --> 00:00:29,001
In April of 2003, many of the world's greatest forensic scientists witnessed the dedication

5
00:00:33,080 --> 00:00:37,000
of a building that promises to be in the vanguard of American law enforcement.

6
00:00:38,060 --> 00:00:41,020
The new FBI crime lab at Quantico, Virginia.

7
00:00:41,021 --> 00:00:50,180
Our dedication theme states, behind every case is a victim, a man, a woman, or a child, and the people who care for them.

8
00:00:50,640 --> 00:00:55,360
We dedicate our efforts and the new FBI laboratory to all of these victims.

9
00:00:56,760 --> 00:01:04,960
At nearly 500,000 square feet, the lab has almost tripled the size of its former home at FBI headquarters in Washington, D.C.

10
00:01:04,961 --> 00:01:11,120
This state-of-the-art facility cost over $150 million to build and equip.

11
00:01:12,440 --> 00:01:15,560
Its intricate construction took four years to complete.

12
00:01:17,520 --> 00:01:21,500
It is, most experts agree, the finest crime lab in the world.

13
00:01:23,080 --> 00:01:30,120
It has been nicknamed Battlestar Galactica, not only for its innovative technology, but also its unique design.

14
00:01:30,121 --> 00:01:35,260
The design is built around the integrity and security of evidence.

15
00:01:35,680 --> 00:01:38,140
That's the most important quality of this building,

16
00:01:38,420 --> 00:01:44,960
to ensure that the evidence we're handling is secure as well as contamination-free.

17
00:01:44,961 --> 00:01:49,680
So the building has been designed with the offices in the front part of the building,

18
00:01:50,200 --> 00:01:53,480
and the back two-thirds of the building is all laboratory space.

19
00:01:54,760 --> 00:01:59,280
The office and laboratory sections have completely separate air handling systems.

20
00:01:59,281 --> 00:02:02,040
ensuring evidence is kept contaminant free.

21
00:02:03,260 --> 00:02:06,140
Evidence is brought into the building via special handling bays,

22
00:02:06,480 --> 00:02:08,960
some of which are large enough to back in a tractor trailer.

23
00:02:10,320 --> 00:02:14,940
All of these efforts are directed towards solving crime with modern forensic science.

24
00:02:15,800 --> 00:02:20,400
Forensic science is the art of finding whatever is left at a crime scene,

25
00:02:20,860 --> 00:02:26,740
developing it in such a way that it can be used to associate it to a person or people who are at the crime scene.

26
00:02:26,741 --> 00:02:36,680
It is an art, and in the hands of the right investigators, it is the strongest kind of tool, because you can't fool science.

27
00:02:37,320 --> 00:02:40,760
To call the massive building a forensics lab is really a misnomer.

28
00:02:41,280 --> 00:02:44,880
It's actually many different labs, all housed together under one roof.

29
00:02:45,440 --> 00:02:49,960
The FBI laboratory is composed of 25 different units.

30
00:02:49,961 --> 00:03:03,160
Those units are basically forensic analysis units doing such exams as latent fingerprints, firearms, DNA analysis, trace evidence, chemistry.

31
00:03:03,760 --> 00:03:12,300
We also have a number of units that are responsible for responding to crime scene, for the collection and preservation of evidence.

32
00:03:13,120 --> 00:03:16,779
The lab employs nearly 700 people, ranging from Ph.D.

33
00:03:16,780 --> 00:03:20,300
level scientists to technicians and lab assistants.

34
00:03:21,580 --> 00:03:28,340
But as large as it is, the lab is merely one division of 13 that comprise the Federal Bureau of Investigation.

35
00:03:29,400 --> 00:03:33,180
Surprisingly, much of its casework originates outside the Bureau itself.

36
00:03:33,181 --> 00:03:39,860
The FBI laboratory actually provides its services to all law enforcement across the United States,

37
00:03:40,020 --> 00:03:42,620
as well as international law enforcement agencies.

38
00:03:43,100 --> 00:03:48,280
Not all crime laboratories have the full range of capabilities that we have in this laboratory.

39
00:03:48,680 --> 00:03:52,000
We provide the analysis, we provide the report,

40
00:03:52,300 --> 00:03:57,920
and we will testify about that report free of charge to any state or local law enforcement agency.

41
00:03:58,860 --> 00:04:04,300
The lab currently receives over 600 pieces of evidence from outside agencies every day.

42
00:04:05,400 --> 00:04:08,200
It has become the busiest crime lab on the planet.

43
00:04:10,340 --> 00:04:12,200
But it wasn't always so.

44
00:04:13,040 --> 00:04:16,140
The lab had its rather humble origins over seven decades ago.

45
00:04:17,360 --> 00:04:20,220
The first impetus for the lab occurred in 1928,

46
00:04:20,740 --> 00:04:23,180
during the infamous Sacco and Vanzetti case.

47
00:04:23,181 --> 00:04:31,960
In that case, for the first time, forensic evidence was introduced in court in the form of bullets fired from the suspect's gun.

48
00:04:34,220 --> 00:04:34,920
The U.S.

49
00:04:34,921 --> 00:04:39,180
Bureau of Investigation, as it was then known, wasn't directly involved in the case.

50
00:04:39,980 --> 00:04:44,100
The Federal Bureau of Investigation is as close to you as your nearest telephone.

51
00:04:44,400 --> 00:04:46,340
But its recently appointed director, J.

52
00:04:46,341 --> 00:04:50,020
Edgar Hoover, was intrigued by the possibilities that forensics presented.

53
00:04:50,021 --> 00:05:00,080
He asked Special Agent Charles Appel to attend classes at the nation's first crime lab at Northwestern University, run by Colonel Calvin Goddard.

54
00:05:01,880 --> 00:05:11,556
Upon returning to Washington, Appel wrote to Hoover proposing a separate division for the handling of so-called crime prevention work, under which a criminological

55
00:05:11,557 --> 00:05:13,040
laboratory can be placed.

56
00:05:15,420 --> 00:05:17,820
Hoover asked Appel to spearhead this effort.

57
00:05:17,821 --> 00:05:19,820
He wrote back to Hoover,

58
00:05:19,821 --> 00:05:24,820
I believe the Bureau should be the clearinghouse for all information which may be needed in criminological work.

59
00:05:26,820 --> 00:05:32,820
All police departments in the future will look to the Bureau for information of this kind as a routine thing.

60
00:05:34,820 --> 00:05:43,820
On September 14, 1932, Appel reported to Hoover that Room 802 in the old Southern Railway building was ready to begin duty as a crime lab.

61
00:05:43,821 --> 00:05:45,580
as a crime lab.

62
00:05:45,581 --> 00:05:48,880
The equipment on that day consisted of an ultraviolet light

63
00:05:48,881 --> 00:05:51,940
machine for inspecting handwriting,

64
00:05:51,941 --> 00:05:55,640
a machine to examine gun barrels, and one microscope

65
00:05:55,641 --> 00:05:57,480
on loan from its manufacturer.

66
00:05:57,481 --> 00:06:01,060
Its staff was a single agent, Charles Appel.

67
00:06:01,061 --> 00:06:02,960
The crime lab started in a lounge.

68
00:06:02,961 --> 00:06:04,760
It was one of the few places in the building that

69
00:06:04,761 --> 00:06:06,680
had available running water.

70
00:06:06,681 --> 00:06:08,660
And its capabilities were really limited.

71
00:06:08,661 --> 00:06:10,060
But its primary purpose was really

72
00:06:10,061 --> 00:06:13,820
to be a public relations tool for the FBI and J. Edgar Hoover.

73
00:06:14,700 --> 00:06:19,260
One of the first cases the new lab tackled was that of the Lindbergh baby kidnapping.

74
00:06:20,640 --> 00:06:24,940
Through handwriting samples, Appel was able to identify Bruno Richard Hauptmann

75
00:06:24,941 --> 00:06:27,360
as the author of the ransom notes for the child.

76
00:06:28,580 --> 00:06:31,500
Hauptmann was convicted and ultimately executed for the crime.

77
00:06:33,100 --> 00:06:37,660
Over the next few years, the lab expanded and was moved into the Justice Department building.

78
00:06:37,661 --> 00:06:46,140
In 1936, it was among the first labs to test the new polygraph, or lie detector.

79
00:06:46,141 --> 00:06:49,960
One year later they began spectrographic analysis.

80
00:06:49,961 --> 00:06:55,460
This process used a beam of refracted light that was shown through a suspected mixture.

81
00:06:55,461 --> 00:07:00,060
As it passed through, the chemical composition of the mixture would absorb specific wavelengths

82
00:07:00,061 --> 00:07:01,940
of the light.

83
00:07:01,941 --> 00:07:06,940
The remaining light would then create a signature display of the mixture's chemical makeup.

84
00:07:06,941 --> 00:07:13,060
In 1940, the lab was designated a full-fledged division of the Bureau, with its own director

85
00:07:13,061 --> 00:07:16,100
in charge.

86
00:07:16,101 --> 00:07:21,400
During World War II, the lab supported the Bureau's prime effort in thwarting espionage.

87
00:07:21,401 --> 00:07:26,180
By developing a method to materialize invisible handwriting, it was instrumental in the capture

88
00:07:26,181 --> 00:07:30,220
of eight Nazi saboteurs who had landed on the beaches of Long Island, New York.

89
00:07:31,540 --> 00:07:36,260
In the 50s and 60s, the lab kept pace with the burgeoning growth of forensic science,

90
00:07:36,860 --> 00:07:41,360
including the creation of the serology unit, which through chemical tests could determine

91
00:07:41,361 --> 00:07:42,360
human blood types.

92
00:07:43,860 --> 00:07:47,540
By the 1970s, the field of forensics was virtually exploding.

93
00:07:48,280 --> 00:07:51,340
To stay current, the lab was again compelled to expand.

94
00:07:52,280 --> 00:07:57,660
This time, the inevitable move was to yet another office building with more space,

95
00:07:58,180 --> 00:08:00,420
FBI headquarters in Washington, D.C.

96
00:08:00,680 --> 00:08:06,760
Starting in probably the mid-'70s, crime labs had become vitally important crime-fighting tools.

97
00:08:07,420 --> 00:08:12,140
What has been shown over and over and over is that physical evidence of any kind

98
00:08:12,141 --> 00:08:16,540
is far more accurate than any kind of eyewitness description.

99
00:08:16,541 --> 00:08:22,540
And good prosecutors would much prefer to have forensic evidence than to have eyewitnesses.

100
00:08:24,540 --> 00:08:30,740
The lab's remarkable growth over its first five decades was based on a simple but forward-looking philosophy.

101
00:08:31,340 --> 00:08:35,200
It embraced new technologies and adapted them to fighting crime.

102
00:08:35,740 --> 00:08:37,280
And they've done it with great success.

103
00:08:37,560 --> 00:08:42,760
They were able to say these hairs match pretty much to the exclusion of any other hair.

104
00:08:43,100 --> 00:08:45,680
They were able to use chemistry in crime fighting.

105
00:08:45,681 --> 00:08:56,520
They were able to figure out how to use the DNA technology, not just to convict people, but how to find people who had been wrongly convicted.

106
00:08:57,540 --> 00:09:04,340
But despite its best efforts, one stumbling block had always hindered the lab from being recognized as truly world class.

107
00:09:05,260 --> 00:09:10,560
The FBI for a long time was inhibited by its facilities.

108
00:09:10,561 --> 00:09:18,200
The odd thing was that the FBI crime lab was the accrediting agency for all national crime labs.

109
00:09:18,420 --> 00:09:24,440
The FBI crime lab wasn't accredited and really couldn't be in those facilities because it was too cramped.

110
00:09:24,540 --> 00:09:27,240
It didn't have the ability to have what they call clean rooms.

111
00:09:27,480 --> 00:09:29,520
There were all kinds of things they couldn't do.

112
00:09:30,080 --> 00:09:36,380
I can remember entering the FBI laboratory as an agent in 1987.

113
00:09:36,381 --> 00:09:43,420
and at that time there was already talk about needing to move out of headquarters

114
00:09:43,421 --> 00:09:48,640
and into a building designed specifically for the FBI laboratory.

115
00:09:49,640 --> 00:09:53,600
In 1997, Congress approved funding for the new crime lab.

116
00:09:54,680 --> 00:09:58,360
Construction followed and now we're in the building.

117
00:09:59,400 --> 00:10:04,280
While the new building itself is impressive, alone it would be nothing but an empty shell.

118
00:10:04,281 --> 00:10:08,480
for the real heart of the lab beats inside its polished steel walls

119
00:10:08,481 --> 00:10:12,780
and the scientists and technicians who make up its highly specialized units

120
00:10:12,781 --> 00:10:17,880
and who daily match wits with those bent on destruction.

121
00:10:20,480 --> 00:10:24,760
The FBI Crime Lab's hallways are each nearly a quarter mile in length.

122
00:10:25,920 --> 00:10:28,880
The FBI Crime Lab will return on Modern Marvels.

123
00:10:30,120 --> 00:10:33,880
We now return to the FBI Crime Lab on Modern Marvels.

124
00:10:34,280 --> 00:10:40,680
The evidence that arrives at the FBI lab is often the remains of a violent crime.

125
00:10:41,780 --> 00:10:43,800
Robbery, rape, or murder.

126
00:10:44,760 --> 00:10:46,300
Frequently, a firearm is involved.

127
00:10:47,400 --> 00:10:51,180
In such cases, the evidence goes to one of the oldest of the lab's 25 units,

128
00:10:51,960 --> 00:10:53,400
firearms and tool marks.

129
00:10:54,240 --> 00:10:58,440
Firearms Tool Marks Unit has been in existence roughly since the beginning of the lab,

130
00:10:58,520 --> 00:10:59,980
which was established in 1932.

131
00:11:00,640 --> 00:11:04,180
On a fundamental basis, it's still the same kinds of techniques we use.

132
00:11:04,280 --> 00:11:08,500
back in the 1930s. Comparison microscopes, looking at ammunition components,

133
00:11:08,501 --> 00:11:11,380
were sometimes called the blacksmiths of the laboratory.

134
00:11:12,120 --> 00:11:16,080
But the analyses these blacksmiths conduct are deadly serious.

135
00:11:16,860 --> 00:11:23,920
The core examination is looking at firearms and the ammunition components that could possibly

136
00:11:23,921 --> 00:11:25,004
be linked to them.

137
00:11:25,005 --> 00:11:29,760
That is, we get in a firearm or several, we get in cartridge cases from a crime

138
00:11:29,761 --> 00:11:30,461
scene.

139
00:11:30,462 --> 00:11:35,660
We get in bullets from a body or from the crime scene in a wall, and we take those

140
00:11:35,661 --> 00:11:39,680
bullets, we take those cartridge cases, we perform some tests, and we try to decide,

141
00:11:40,240 --> 00:11:43,020
did they come from this gun or did they not, or can we not tell?

142
00:11:44,360 --> 00:11:48,120
How that occurs has to do with the nature of how firearms are manufactured.

143
00:11:49,140 --> 00:11:53,400
Very often the question arises, just how do you link a bullet to a particular barrel or

144
00:11:53,401 --> 00:11:54,260
a particular gun?

145
00:11:54,261 --> 00:11:57,800
The answer starts out with the nature of the inside of a barrel, where

146
00:11:57,801 --> 00:12:00,470
there are grooves that are manufactured into the barrel.

147
00:12:00,471 --> 00:12:02,520
You can often think of taking your fingers,

148
00:12:03,000 --> 00:12:05,635
moving them down the barrel, and they'll rotate.

149
00:12:05,636 --> 00:12:08,380
The reason they rotate is just like when you throw

150
00:12:08,381 --> 00:12:12,454
a football, you want it to spiral because it gives it stability.

151
00:12:12,455 --> 00:12:14,300
The cut-in grooves and raised

152
00:12:14,301 --> 00:12:19,580
portions, or lands, enables the matching of bullets with gun barrels with remarkable precision.

153
00:12:20,860 --> 00:12:25,880
Every gun that we've ever seen, that's ever been researched by others, will leave its own

154
00:12:25,881 --> 00:12:30,840
individual unique microscopic marks. If I can use a model here for illustration, you

155
00:12:30,841 --> 00:12:32,421
can see this little valley here.

156
00:12:32,422 --> 00:12:35,040
That's an impression from part of the interior of the

157
00:12:35,041 --> 00:12:41,840
barrel. This portion, this raised portion, is from another part. Now, if we have ten

158
00:12:41,841 --> 00:12:48,940
of these different gross marks on a bullet, but only five land and grooves inside a barrel,

159
00:12:48,941 --> 00:12:52,667
then obviously it didn't come from that barrel. And we can eliminate it right off.

160
00:12:52,668 --> 00:12:53,368
On the

161
00:12:53,369 --> 00:12:57,060
On the other hand, if everything matches, we start looking at the smaller microscopic marks,

162
00:12:57,180 --> 00:13:01,980
and it's from those that we can actually individualize a particular bullet to a particular barrel.

163
00:13:03,320 --> 00:13:08,340
The microscopic markings are the results of specific wear patterns unique to each barrel.

164
00:13:09,480 --> 00:13:14,680
To obtain a sample bullet from a suspected weapon, the unit has a specialized piece of equipment.

165
00:13:15,180 --> 00:13:20,880
We take that gun to a water tank, a big eight-foot-long tank, fire into it.

166
00:13:20,881 --> 00:13:27,160
The bullet will slow down very fast, drop to the bottom of the tank, then we pluck it

167
00:13:27,161 --> 00:13:33,440
out and we take that test-fired bullet and we compare that to a bullet from a crime scene.

168
00:13:33,441 --> 00:13:38,220
We can look at those small little striations or scratch marks on those bullets side by

169
00:13:38,221 --> 00:13:39,640
side at the same time.

170
00:13:39,641 --> 00:13:43,200
Again, if there's a significant amount of correspondence, we can say they're from the

171
00:13:43,201 --> 00:13:45,440
same gun.

172
00:13:45,441 --> 00:13:49,840
As part of their charge, the Firearms Unit also maintains a remarkable collection of

173
00:13:49,841 --> 00:13:50,840
weapons.

174
00:13:50,841 --> 00:13:56,260
Our reference firearms collection is the largest forensic collection of its type in the United States.

175
00:13:56,420 --> 00:13:58,700
We have close to 6,000 weapons in here.

176
00:13:59,200 --> 00:14:02,220
Our firearms reference collection is not a museum.

177
00:14:02,480 --> 00:14:05,820
This is an actual working collection, and it has many purposes.

178
00:14:05,880 --> 00:14:11,200
We use them for training purposes, to strip them down and look at the parts and see how they work.

179
00:14:11,320 --> 00:14:15,680
Very often we'll need to tear down an evidence gun, but we don't want to start with that.

180
00:14:15,860 --> 00:14:19,300
We want to take a duplicate from our collection and tear it down first.

181
00:14:19,301 --> 00:14:24,740
We won't be damaging or altering in any way an evidence gun any more than is absolutely necessary.

182
00:14:26,100 --> 00:14:30,160
Within the vast collection are many firearms of historical significance.

183
00:14:30,740 --> 00:14:34,160
We have a revolver that was used by John Dillinger, the gangster.

184
00:14:34,700 --> 00:14:40,700
We have two semi-automatic pistols used by Pretty Boy Floyd, a semi-automatic pistol by Alvin Creepy Karpus.

185
00:14:41,080 --> 00:14:45,020
And all these weapons, to my knowledge, are in still good operating condition.

186
00:14:45,021 --> 00:14:51,800
Working with these tools of violence, the scientists of the firearms unit strive to make a positive impact.

187
00:14:53,120 --> 00:14:56,440
We do make a definite social contribution here.

188
00:14:56,800 --> 00:14:59,980
We're helping to get to the truth in a courtroom.

189
00:15:00,860 --> 00:15:04,320
Helping to put the bad guys away, helping to set the innocent free.

190
00:15:04,740 --> 00:15:06,880
And I know it sounds hokey, but that's a good feeling.

191
00:15:08,960 --> 00:15:13,360
Criminals for whom a firearm isn't powerful enough choose a different type of weapon.

192
00:15:15,020 --> 00:15:18,820
It's then that the lab's explosive unit is called in.

193
00:15:21,440 --> 00:15:26,680
The basic components of a bomb would be to have some sort of energetic material

194
00:15:26,681 --> 00:15:32,300
which would provide that explosive force or release of energy

195
00:15:32,301 --> 00:15:34,740
and then some means to initiate that.

196
00:15:34,960 --> 00:15:37,680
Those are the key ingredients in any bomb.

197
00:15:38,360 --> 00:15:41,980
But for the members of the explosives unit, it's rarely that simple.

198
00:15:41,981 --> 00:15:48,980
Most commonly we run into what we call improvised explosive devices or IEDs.

199
00:15:48,981 --> 00:15:53,980
And these IEDs we also will refer to most commonly as homemade bombs.

200
00:15:53,981 --> 00:16:02,980
And it's the homemade bomb that we are most concerned with because these are the things that are completely foreign to us

201
00:16:02,981 --> 00:16:08,260
and can have booby-trap features or certain trick designs

202
00:16:08,261 --> 00:16:11,760
that make them very dangerous for us here in the laboratory.

203
00:16:12,380 --> 00:16:15,740
So the homemade bomb is not only our most common,

204
00:16:15,840 --> 00:16:18,080
but our most dangerous or threatening device.

205
00:16:19,840 --> 00:16:23,180
Probably the largest case ever tackled by the explosives unit

206
00:16:23,181 --> 00:16:27,700
was the crash of Pan Am 103 over Lockerbie, Scotland in 1988.

207
00:16:28,640 --> 00:16:33,820
Pan Am 103 was clearly the largest crime scene in history.

208
00:16:33,960 --> 00:16:35,800
It was almost 800 square miles.

209
00:16:36,580 --> 00:16:40,000
And one of the things that has never been reported

210
00:16:40,001 --> 00:16:44,800
was how the entire town of Lockerbie became detectives,

211
00:16:44,920 --> 00:16:48,240
picking things up, marking where they found them, and handing them in.

212
00:16:48,440 --> 00:16:51,440
They developed bags and bags and bags of evidence.

213
00:16:51,720 --> 00:16:54,880
And it all came to the FBI crime lab in Washington.

214
00:16:54,881 --> 00:16:59,398
him. One of the first things looked for was the evidence of high explosives damage.

215
00:16:59,399 --> 00:17:00,160
Because of its

216
00:17:00,161 --> 00:17:06,058
characteristics, it can't be attributed merely to the breakup of an aircraft.

217
00:17:06,059 --> 00:17:07,360
It's high energy,

218
00:17:07,920 --> 00:17:13,803
explosive force at work. It creates knife-like edges to the metal.

219
00:17:13,804 --> 00:17:16,120
It causes unique rippling,

220
00:17:16,260 --> 00:17:19,080
which we sometimes refer to as gas washing.

221
00:17:19,081 --> 00:17:21,900
Examiners from our unit recognize this high

222
00:17:21,901 --> 00:17:28,420
explosive damage on these particular pieces that went to a particular cargo container.

223
00:17:28,800 --> 00:17:35,380
And from that, they were then able to determine certain pieces of luggage exhibited this high

224
00:17:35,381 --> 00:17:40,600
explosive damage and knew that the device had to have been in that piece of luggage.

225
00:17:41,400 --> 00:17:45,140
Once it was established that a bomb had indeed brought down Flight 103,

226
00:17:46,080 --> 00:17:48,900
it was sheer tenacity that led investigators to the bombers.

227
00:17:49,840 --> 00:17:54,380
There was an investigator named Tom Thurman working in the explosives unit.

228
00:17:55,580 --> 00:18:00,160
And he found a thumbnail piece of plastic that he knew he had seen before,

229
00:18:00,380 --> 00:18:01,940
but he didn't know where he had seen it.

230
00:18:02,260 --> 00:18:07,480
And he took that piece to a friend of his, another expert in this kind of materials,

231
00:18:08,140 --> 00:18:10,820
and that expert looked at it and said, you know, I've seen that before too.

232
00:18:11,180 --> 00:18:12,580
He said, I know where I've seen it.

233
00:18:12,581 --> 00:18:17,620
And he went in the back and he brought out another unexploded bomb.

234
00:18:17,621 --> 00:18:20,660
that had been found in Africa.

235
00:18:21,540 --> 00:18:22,880
And they took the back off,

236
00:18:23,180 --> 00:18:28,240
and this thumbnail piece of a timer was identical to the timer used in these other bombs.

237
00:18:28,600 --> 00:18:30,500
They knew who had made these other bombs.

238
00:18:31,820 --> 00:18:34,260
Years of hard work had finally paid off.

239
00:18:34,940 --> 00:18:37,680
Two members of Libyan intelligence were charged with the bombing.

240
00:18:38,660 --> 00:18:41,680
After the Libyan government refused to turn them over to world court,

241
00:18:42,240 --> 00:18:44,720
they were tried and found guilty in absentia.

242
00:18:47,620 --> 00:18:53,580
In today's current climate, and its heightened risk of terrorism, the explosives unit is more important than ever.

243
00:18:53,581 --> 00:19:04,080
We're working to try to help solve some of the most violent and cowardly acts of criminal or terrorist activity that occur.

244
00:19:04,340 --> 00:19:12,780
It's exciting in that it's like a major puzzle that has all these varieties of pieces,

245
00:19:12,781 --> 00:19:18,640
and we try to piece this together and determine how everything fits.

246
00:19:18,840 --> 00:19:23,580
And the greatest satisfaction is in putting these people behind bars

247
00:19:23,581 --> 00:19:26,340
after they've committed such a violent act.

248
00:19:27,480 --> 00:19:31,040
While the explosive unit's cases are often larger than life,

249
00:19:31,720 --> 00:19:35,480
other units must look closer in order to solve their mysteries.

250
00:19:35,481 --> 00:19:42,480
The FBI Crime Lab annually conducts about one million forensic analyses.

251
00:19:42,481 --> 00:19:46,480
The FBI Crime Lab will return on Modern Marvels.

252
00:19:46,481 --> 00:19:52,480
We now return to the FBI Crime Lab on Modern Marvels.

253
00:19:52,481 --> 00:20:00,480
Often, the FBI's ability to single out one person from a crowd can determine success or failure.

254
00:20:00,481 --> 00:20:01,281
or failure.

255
00:20:01,282 --> 00:20:06,740
In cases of this type, the latent print unit is the granddaddy of them all.

256
00:20:07,520 --> 00:20:12,100
If you look back in a lot of the history books clear back before Christ, the Chinese had

257
00:20:12,101 --> 00:20:16,058
recognized that there was friction ridge patterns on the end joints of the fingers.

258
00:20:16,059 --> 00:20:16,759
Not that

259
00:20:16,760 --> 00:20:19,540
they knew to do anything with them, they just recognized that they were there.

260
00:20:20,500 --> 00:20:25,140
But well into the 19th century, an entirely different system of personal identification

261
00:20:25,141 --> 00:20:26,600
was deeply entrenched.

262
00:20:27,160 --> 00:20:28,880
The accepted means

263
00:20:28,881 --> 00:20:31,180
was a French system

264
00:20:31,181 --> 00:20:32,460
called the Bertilien method,

265
00:20:32,700 --> 00:20:35,360
a way of measuring body parts,

266
00:20:35,920 --> 00:20:37,760
the distance from the tip of your finger

267
00:20:37,761 --> 00:20:39,320
to the point of your elbow,

268
00:20:39,660 --> 00:20:42,000
the girth of your head above the eyebrows.

269
00:20:42,540 --> 00:20:44,600
There were 22 different measurements used,

270
00:20:44,680 --> 00:20:45,760
and it was based on the premise

271
00:20:45,761 --> 00:20:47,280
that no two people would have

272
00:20:47,281 --> 00:20:48,620
the same Bertilien measurements.

273
00:20:48,800 --> 00:20:50,160
And that's pretty much the way it was

274
00:20:50,161 --> 00:20:52,360
until 1905,

275
00:20:52,361 --> 00:20:54,020
when a man named William West

276
00:20:54,021 --> 00:20:56,480
was arrested and sent to Fort Leavenworth Prison.

277
00:20:57,080 --> 00:20:59,080
And they took his Bertillian measurements,

278
00:20:59,620 --> 00:21:00,780
and they looked in the file,

279
00:21:00,980 --> 00:21:03,960
and they discovered there already was a William West

280
00:21:03,961 --> 00:21:05,820
at Fort Leavenworth Prison

281
00:21:05,821 --> 00:21:08,740
who had precisely the same Bertillian measurements.

282
00:21:09,340 --> 00:21:11,360
Only thing different about the two of them

283
00:21:11,361 --> 00:21:12,380
was their fingerprints.

284
00:21:13,000 --> 00:21:15,440
And that was the day fingerprinting became

285
00:21:15,441 --> 00:21:19,240
the chosen method of identifying a person.

286
00:21:20,880 --> 00:21:23,960
Even before the lab opened its doors in 1932,

287
00:21:24,020 --> 00:21:27,900
The Bureau was involved in fingerprint identification and indexing.

288
00:21:28,580 --> 00:21:33,460
The FBI recognized the benefit to using fingerprints as a means of positive identification.

289
00:21:33,880 --> 00:21:38,180
So in 1924, we started and created the first fingerprint files.

290
00:21:38,680 --> 00:21:44,560
It wasn't until 1933 that we began using latent prints off of crime scene evidence

291
00:21:44,561 --> 00:21:47,920
to try to support criminal investigative matters.

292
00:21:48,820 --> 00:21:53,040
This breakthrough was the result of discovering that latent, or hidden fingerprints,

293
00:21:53,041 --> 00:21:54,900
are often left at a crime scene.

294
00:21:55,780 --> 00:21:58,360
If you'll think of it as a series of ridges and valleys,

295
00:21:58,840 --> 00:22:01,560
it's the ridges which are the protruding areas of skin.

296
00:22:01,700 --> 00:22:03,500
That comes into contact with that object.

297
00:22:04,040 --> 00:22:06,640
Well, those friction ridges have a series of sweat pores.

298
00:22:07,220 --> 00:22:11,360
It is the perspiration from your body that aligns along those ridges

299
00:22:11,361 --> 00:22:13,220
so that when you touch an object,

300
00:22:13,221 --> 00:22:17,120
it's that perspiration which gets transferred onto the object

301
00:22:17,121 --> 00:22:18,900
in the outline of your fingerprint.

302
00:22:18,901 --> 00:22:23,080
That perspiration is about 98 to 99 percent water.

303
00:22:23,260 --> 00:22:29,520
The rest of it has chemical composites of your body chemistry, amino acids, riboflavones, and so forth.

304
00:22:29,960 --> 00:22:36,100
It's those elements that we utilize to try to develop those latent prints on any object that you touch.

305
00:22:37,880 --> 00:22:45,600
Fingerprints moved firmly into the national psyche when public enemy number one, John Dillinger, was killed outside a Chicago movie theater.

306
00:22:45,601 --> 00:22:50,840
And there was a real question of whether it was John Dillinger

307
00:22:50,841 --> 00:22:54,940
or whether John Dillinger, this brilliant mastermind,

308
00:22:55,000 --> 00:22:58,360
had known about this assassination plot, that they were going to kill him,

309
00:22:58,580 --> 00:23:00,160
and had sent somebody in his place.

310
00:23:00,820 --> 00:23:03,660
Dillinger had made every effort to change his appearance.

311
00:23:03,820 --> 00:23:06,520
Just like in the movies, he dipped his fingerprints in acid,

312
00:23:06,660 --> 00:23:09,480
he had had some rudimentary plastic surgery.

313
00:23:09,860 --> 00:23:12,680
There are people today who will still tell you that wasn't John Dillinger.

314
00:23:13,680 --> 00:23:18,720
Dillinger did in fact succeed in blurring the prints at the tips of his fingers, but to no avail.

315
00:23:19,360 --> 00:23:24,100
The fact is that your uniqueness can be identified to the second and third joints of the finger,

316
00:23:24,101 --> 00:23:29,560
so all we had to do in that case was look below the first joint of the finger to the second joint,

317
00:23:29,960 --> 00:23:33,120
and we could positively identify him as John Dillinger.

318
00:23:34,560 --> 00:23:40,680
As forensic science advanced, the number of techniques to develop latent fingerprints also expanded rapidly.

319
00:23:40,681 --> 00:23:47,020
In the 1930s, the lab had only three processes employing chemical powders or liquids.

320
00:23:48,020 --> 00:23:50,540
Today, there are nearly 80 different processes,

321
00:23:51,260 --> 00:23:54,740
from cyanoacrylate fumes to alternate light sources

322
00:23:54,741 --> 00:23:57,500
that the lab can bring to bear in their search for a suspect.

323
00:23:58,660 --> 00:24:00,160
Many are highly specialized.

324
00:24:01,040 --> 00:24:03,880
Occasionally, we will have evidence that would have blood on it,

325
00:24:03,960 --> 00:24:05,860
especially in a homicide-type case.

326
00:24:06,380 --> 00:24:09,660
And that poses an interesting perspective to us

327
00:24:09,661 --> 00:24:13,000
because now we have another substance on the evidence itself.

328
00:24:14,360 --> 00:24:17,320
To bring out the latent prints captured in the blood stain,

329
00:24:17,920 --> 00:24:21,780
the unit uses a special chemical process called amido black,

330
00:24:22,120 --> 00:24:24,740
which reacts with the actual proteins in the blood itself.

331
00:24:25,720 --> 00:24:29,480
After it is rinsed off, the amido black clings to the bloody fingerprints,

332
00:24:30,080 --> 00:24:31,600
revealing them in amazing detail.

333
00:24:33,780 --> 00:24:37,420
Another modern process was used for the first time in the 1970s

334
00:24:37,421 --> 00:24:40,280
while searching for proof of World War II crimes.

335
00:24:42,460 --> 00:24:46,080
Verlarian Trefa was a bishop in the Romanian Orthodox Church of America.

336
00:24:47,000 --> 00:24:50,680
In 1975, deportation hearings began against him,

337
00:24:51,100 --> 00:24:54,140
claiming that Trefa had covered up his past in the Iron Guard,

338
00:24:54,720 --> 00:24:56,260
Romania's pro-Nazi party.

339
00:24:57,200 --> 00:24:58,620
Trefa denied this accusation,

340
00:24:59,120 --> 00:25:01,280
confident that time had buried any proof.

341
00:25:01,500 --> 00:25:03,920
The West German government had a postcard

342
00:25:03,921 --> 00:25:07,280
that had been mailed in the early 1940s to Himmler.

343
00:25:07,420 --> 00:25:12,180
And if they could make the association that it was in fact a postcard sent by Bishop Trefa,

344
00:25:12,840 --> 00:25:14,980
it would prove that he was a Nazi collaborator.

345
00:25:15,640 --> 00:25:19,040
The problem was that the German government considered this an historical document.

346
00:25:19,180 --> 00:25:22,240
And while they were willing to loan it to the FBI for investigation,

347
00:25:22,380 --> 00:25:29,040
they were not willing to let the FBI use any method on the card that would have caused any damage.

348
00:25:29,260 --> 00:25:33,040
The chemical processes of the day all would have ruined the postcard.

349
00:25:34,220 --> 00:25:37,080
So they turned to a then radical new technology.

350
00:25:37,420 --> 00:25:42,520
The lab hoped their new laser might reveal a fingerprint on the decades-old card.

351
00:25:42,580 --> 00:25:49,200
We detected a latent print on that fingerprint card 41 years after it was put into that bank vault,

352
00:25:49,380 --> 00:25:51,440
and it was identified with Bishop Trefa.

353
00:25:52,780 --> 00:25:55,840
Trefa was caught in his lie and eventually deported.

354
00:25:57,660 --> 00:26:04,880
Today's sophisticated lasers and other alternate light sources continue to play a major role in the casework of the latent print unit.

355
00:26:04,881 --> 00:26:09,880
The laser that we use in the latent print unit is a 30 watt laser.

356
00:26:09,881 --> 00:26:12,880
The laser beam will come out into a fiber optic.

357
00:26:12,881 --> 00:26:18,880
The fiber optic will have a diffracting lens at the end which will disperse that laser light across the display table.

358
00:26:18,881 --> 00:26:22,880
We'll simply take the evidence and looking through special filters,

359
00:26:22,881 --> 00:26:28,880
the latent print will luminesce at a higher frequency than the surrounding area on the object.

360
00:26:28,881 --> 00:26:32,880
This is due to fingerprints having a faint inherent luminescence,

361
00:26:32,881 --> 00:26:37,880
which can be vastly amplified by the use of a powerful laser.

362
00:26:37,881 --> 00:26:42,880
And although the latent print unit has pioneered many such technologies,

363
00:26:42,881 --> 00:26:46,880
it's still constantly looking to update its methods.

364
00:26:46,881 --> 00:26:51,880
Today we pretty much deal with a two-dimensional impression of fingerprints.

365
00:26:51,881 --> 00:26:54,880
What we know is that the finger is three-dimensional.

366
00:26:54,881 --> 00:26:57,880
The ridges and valleys are a three-dimensional configuration.

367
00:26:57,881 --> 00:26:58,904
configuration.

368
00:26:58,905 --> 00:27:05,120
We are moving rapidly to that arena of using three-dimensional imagery of the finger,

369
00:27:05,360 --> 00:27:10,500
as well as being able to capture three-dimensional images of the latent print on crime scene evidence.

370
00:27:11,460 --> 00:27:14,780
While fingerprints have a long history as a forensic tool,

371
00:27:16,020 --> 00:27:21,020
another of the lab's units deals with the fingerprints of the 21st century, DNA.

372
00:27:21,620 --> 00:27:25,340
DNA is the biological equivalent of fingerprints.

373
00:27:25,760 --> 00:27:29,200
They've never, never found two people with the same fingerprints,

374
00:27:29,740 --> 00:27:32,200
and they have never found two people with the same DNA.

375
00:27:33,920 --> 00:27:37,340
DNA determines the unique differences in how we all look.

376
00:27:38,060 --> 00:27:41,500
It's stored in virtually every cell in our bodies as genetic code.

377
00:27:42,240 --> 00:27:46,920
Even before the first conviction in the U.S. using DNA evidence in 1988,

378
00:27:46,921 --> 00:27:51,100
the FBI lab was in the forefront of DNA forensic research.

379
00:27:51,101 --> 00:27:59,200
Back in the mid-1980s, the FBI laboratory decided that we needed to use the DNA technology

380
00:27:59,201 --> 00:28:02,880
that was first being used in Manchester, England.

381
00:28:03,140 --> 00:28:08,440
So we spent three years developing the technology of DNA

382
00:28:08,441 --> 00:28:13,060
for application to rapes and homicides and other violent crimes.

383
00:28:13,061 --> 00:28:19,540
and in December of 1988, we began to apply that technology to our casework.

384
00:28:20,400 --> 00:28:25,580
Even with the success, the FBI lab continued in its efforts to refine DNA testing.

385
00:28:26,180 --> 00:28:27,740
This led to another breakthrough.

386
00:28:28,660 --> 00:28:32,980
There are actually two distinct types of DNA which could be exploited as evidence.

387
00:28:33,540 --> 00:28:38,640
Nuclear DNA is found in the nucleus of a cell, which is sort of like the yolk of an egg,

388
00:28:38,641 --> 00:28:43,000
and mitochondrial DNA is found in the outer layers of the cell,

389
00:28:43,180 --> 00:28:45,160
sort of like the egg white of an egg.

390
00:28:45,780 --> 00:28:50,360
Nuclear DNA is the substance which encodes physical characteristics

391
00:28:50,361 --> 00:28:52,220
such as eye color or hair color,

392
00:28:52,500 --> 00:28:55,580
but there are only two copies of that DNA in cells.

393
00:28:56,340 --> 00:28:58,220
Mitochondrial DNA, on the other hand,

394
00:28:58,400 --> 00:29:00,900
is found in much higher copy number in cells.

395
00:29:01,760 --> 00:29:04,780
This discrepancy in copy number was a critical discovery.

396
00:29:06,020 --> 00:29:07,860
Because it was so much more plentiful,

397
00:29:07,861 --> 00:29:13,980
Mitochondrial technology now made DNA testing possible on such items as bones and other evidence,

398
00:29:13,981 --> 00:29:16,760
which had been resistant to nuclear DNA testing.

399
00:29:18,700 --> 00:29:26,280
In many cases, the evidence we receive from crime scenes has been exposed to the environment for a long time.

400
00:29:26,600 --> 00:29:32,020
So that evidence will be degraded, much like food spoils when you leave it outside of a refrigerator.

401
00:29:32,021 --> 00:29:41,680
In those cases, we perform mitochondrial DNA analysis because there are more copies of mitochondrial DNA present in that evidence to begin with.

402
00:29:43,360 --> 00:29:51,500
The continued success of both nuclear and mitochondrial DNA testing led to the creation of the National DNA Index System.

403
00:29:51,501 --> 00:30:03,100
The National DNA Index System is a nationwide network of more than 170 crime labs that are hooked together that share DNA information.

404
00:30:03,880 --> 00:30:11,100
Today, there are more than 1.5 million convicted offender profiles in the National DNA Index System.

405
00:30:11,360 --> 00:30:17,240
We have solved well over 6,000 cases using this DNA technology.

406
00:30:17,241 --> 00:30:23,160
With a national online database to share both nuclear and mitochondrial information,

407
00:30:23,740 --> 00:30:29,640
the fingerprints of the 21st century will be identifying criminal suspects with more and more frequency.

408
00:30:30,180 --> 00:30:37,380
The most exciting thing is that I get to see my work help victims of crimes and their families.

409
00:30:37,940 --> 00:30:41,220
I get to see that people have been helped by the work that's done here in the laboratory.

410
00:30:41,221 --> 00:30:50,360
But when there is no DNA available, other units must work their high-tech magic to unlock the evidence's hidden mysteries.

411
00:30:52,280 --> 00:30:59,800
The FBI has the fingerprints of 44 million people, or 440 million separate fingerprints on file.

412
00:31:01,220 --> 00:31:04,620
The FBI Crime Lab will return on Modern Marvels.

413
00:31:06,380 --> 00:31:10,300
We now return to the FBI Crime Lab on Modern Marvels.

414
00:31:11,220 --> 00:31:21,220
Incriminating evidence often arrives at the FBI lab hidden on or inside everyday objects, like clothing or pieces of carpet.

415
00:31:21,221 --> 00:31:28,220
To develop these almost imperceptible clues, the lab relies on its chemistry and trace evidence units.

416
00:31:28,221 --> 00:31:34,220
It's impossible to be in an environment without both leaving hair and fiber evidence that you were there,

417
00:31:34,221 --> 00:31:39,220
but also picking up hairs and fibers from that environment and taking them with you.

418
00:31:39,221 --> 00:31:46,220
When we get evidence in, what we're looking for is the presence of either hairs, fibers, glass or soil.

419
00:31:46,221 --> 00:31:52,220
And what we're actually looking at is whatever item we're examining, something that doesn't belong on it,

420
00:31:52,221 --> 00:31:54,220
what we call foreign to that item.

421
00:31:54,221 --> 00:32:01,220
And then we're trying to associate either those hairs, fibers, glass or soil that don't come from that item to a possible source.

422
00:32:02,220 --> 00:32:07,220
To determine which hair and fiber evidence is of interest in an investigation,

423
00:32:07,221 --> 00:32:09,560
The unit developed its own distinct protocols.

424
00:32:10,000 --> 00:32:13,700
We take that debris that we remove from those items of clothing that were submitted

425
00:32:13,701 --> 00:32:16,560
and look at them under a low-power microscope.

426
00:32:16,840 --> 00:32:20,160
The debris is looked at for hairs and fibers that might be of interest.

427
00:32:20,460 --> 00:32:22,820
Then, if they are consistent to each other,

428
00:32:23,300 --> 00:32:27,640
I would look at them under fluorescent microscopy

429
00:32:27,641 --> 00:32:29,760
to see if they also fluoresce the same way.

430
00:32:30,840 --> 00:32:35,620
If all of these things are consistent between the known fiber and the question fiber,

431
00:32:35,621 --> 00:32:39,620
then I would take it and have it run on Fourier transform infrared spectrophotometry.

432
00:32:39,920 --> 00:32:44,260
The Fourier transform infrared spectrophotometer, or FTIR,

433
00:32:44,680 --> 00:32:46,880
is on the cutting edge of trace evidence analysis.

434
00:32:47,620 --> 00:32:51,140
It can identify the actual chemical structure of the substance being examined

435
00:32:51,141 --> 00:32:54,760
by utilizing the electromagnetic spectrum above visible light.

436
00:32:55,340 --> 00:32:59,360
When coupled to a reference database, it can readily identify unknown compounds.

437
00:33:00,860 --> 00:33:05,420
Basically, FTIR will allow us to determine what the subtype of that fiber is,

438
00:33:05,421 --> 00:33:08,380
Although we've determined earlier on the microscope that it's a nylon,

439
00:33:08,620 --> 00:33:13,520
it'll tell us that it's a nylon 6 or nylon 6-6, a specific type of nylon.

440
00:33:14,220 --> 00:33:17,760
While hair and fiber evidence are staples in cases of a violent nature,

441
00:33:18,480 --> 00:33:21,380
in and of themselves they usually aren't enough to seal a conviction.

442
00:33:22,160 --> 00:33:24,500
But in one chilling case in 1979,

443
00:33:25,140 --> 00:33:28,100
it was the only physical evidence the prosecution had.

444
00:33:29,280 --> 00:33:32,840
It began with people disappearing in the Atlanta metro area.

445
00:33:33,680 --> 00:33:39,220
Their bodies, found disposed of in rural wooded areas, were all connected by a thread.

446
00:33:39,221 --> 00:33:45,040
That case started with bodies being found that had some fibers on them,

447
00:33:45,180 --> 00:33:48,160
and they were able to interrelate victims with those fibers,

448
00:33:48,360 --> 00:33:53,440
so that now they had the idea that the same person was responsible for this string of murders.

449
00:33:53,960 --> 00:33:57,860
After local news reported that common fibers had been found on the victims,

450
00:33:58,620 --> 00:34:01,840
bodies began showing up floating naked in the Chattahoochee River.

451
00:34:02,500 --> 00:34:05,460
an obvious attempt to remove any hair or fiber evidence.

452
00:34:06,760 --> 00:34:09,480
Police soon began staking out bridges in the area,

453
00:34:10,260 --> 00:34:11,120
hoping for a break.

454
00:34:11,580 --> 00:34:13,000
It came late one night.

455
00:34:13,620 --> 00:34:15,760
After hearing a loud splash in the river below,

456
00:34:16,280 --> 00:34:17,920
the police stopped Wayne Williams,

457
00:34:18,500 --> 00:34:22,240
a 23-year-old local music promoter who was driving away.

458
00:34:23,320 --> 00:34:26,340
His claim that he had merely thrown garbage over the side

459
00:34:26,341 --> 00:34:28,200
was belied two days later

460
00:34:28,201 --> 00:34:31,020
when a body washed up just a mile from the bridge.

